+# UsdtYieldVault — pre-deployment review
+
+**Recommendation: do not ship this on Monday.**
+
+Five defects, all reproducible against `src/UsdtYieldVault.sol` exactly as it is
+committed today. Nothing under `src/` was modified. Two of them are fatal on
+their own:
+
+- **F1** — every `deposit()` reverts against the real USDT contract. The vault
+  cannot take a single deposit on mainnet.
+- **F2** — the withdrawal fee is subtracted from the vault's accounting but
+  never withdrawn from Aave and never credited to anyone. It is destroyed. On a
+  200,000 USDT round trip, 600 USDT is permanently unrecoverable.
+- **F3** — Aave interest never reaches depositors. A one-year 100,000 USDT
+  deposit earns 4,256.65 USDT that the depositor cannot claim and nobody can
+  ever retrieve.
+- **F4** — `setDepositFee` has no upper bound. One owner transaction can
+  confiscate 99.99% of a pending deposit, or brick deposits permanently.
+- **F5** — `pause()` freezes withdrawals, so one key can lock every depositor
+  out of their funds with no timelock and no emergency exit.
+
+## Why the existing suite is green
+
+The 39 passing tests and the 100% line/function coverage are both accurate and
+both uninformative here. Coverage records which lines executed; it never records
+whether an assertion could have failed.
+
+Three specific gaps let all five defects through:
+
+1. **Everything is mocked.** `test/mocks/MockUSDT.sol` is an OpenZeppelin ERC20.
+   Real USDT is not. `test/mocks/MockAavePool.sol` mints aTokens 1:1 and never
+   accrues interest. Real Aave does. A mock encodes the assumption; testing
+   against it only re-tests the assumption. F1 and F3 are invisible to any
+   number of mock-based tests.
+2. **No property tied accounting to custody.** Every existing test checks one
+   operation in isolation, and every operation is individually self-consistent.
+   F2 is a per-withdrawal gap between what the vault *says* it holds and what it
+   *actually* holds; it only becomes visible when you state that equality and
+   let a fuzzer drive sequences at it.
+3. **`depositFeeBps` was only ever tested at hand-picked values** — 10, 0, and
+   500. The whole accepted domain is every `uint256`, and the interesting values
+   live at 9,999 and above.
+
+Below, each finding has the command and its verbatim terminal output.
+
+---
+
+## Setup for reproducing
+
+New test files only, all under `test/`:
+
+| File | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `test/VaultMainnetFork.t.sol` | Pinned mainnet fork against real USDT / Aave V3 / aUSDT |
+| `test/VaultInvariant.t.sol` + `test/handlers/VaultHandler.sol` | Handler-driven invariant: accounting == custody |
+| `test/VaultAccountingDrift.t.sol` | Targeted regressions confirming and quantifying F2 |
+| `test/VaultDepositFeeFuzz.t.sol` | Fee-domain fuzz + boundary classification |
+| `test/VaultLiveness.t.sol` | Can a depositor always exit? |
+
+The fork tests default to `https://eth.drpc.org`, which serves archive state at
+block 21,000,000; override with `MAINNET_RPC_URL` if you prefer your own. I
+confirmed the endpoint answers at the pinned block, and confirmed all three
+addresses are what the README claims, before relying on any result from them:
+
