+
+## F1 — Critical. Every deposit reverts against real USDT
+
+### What is wrong
+
+`UsdtYieldVault.sol:93`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.approve(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
+This goes through `IERC20.approve`, which is declared `returns (bool)`. Mainnet
+USDT's `approve` writes **no return data at all**. Solidity's ABI decoder checks
+`returndatasize()` before decoding the `bool`, finds zero bytes, and reverts.
+
+The contract imports `SafeERC20` and uses it for `safeTransferFrom` one line
+earlier — which is why the transfer works and the approve does not. The approve
+was simply missed.
+
+Consequence: `deposit()` is unreachable on mainnet. The vault takes no money.
+It is not a loss-of-funds bug, it is a total-failure-to-function bug — but it
+would be discovered by the first user on Monday, not by us.
+
+### Evidence
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-path test/VaultMainnetFork.t.sol --match-test test_RealUsdtApproveReturnsNoData -vv
+
+Ran 1 test for test/VaultMainnetFork.t.sol:VaultMainnetForkTest
+[FAIL: USDT.approve returned a decodable bool: 0 != 32] test_RealUsdtApproveReturnsNoData() (gas: 39110)
+Logs:
+  USDT.approve return data length: 0
+```
+
+The raw call succeeds; it just returns zero bytes. And the deposit itself:
+
+```
+$ forge test --match-test test_DepositAgainstRealUsdt -vvvv
+
+  [94187] VaultMainnetForkTest::test_DepositAgainstRealUsdt()
+    ├─ [0] VM::prank(alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6])
+    │   └─ ← [Return]
+    ├─ [84174] UsdtYieldVault::deposit(100000000000 [1e11])
+    │   ├─ [43630] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::transferFrom(alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], 100000000000 [1e11])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Transfer(from: alice: [0x328809Bc894f92807417D2dAD6b7C998c1aFdac6], to: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], value: 100000000000 [1e11])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   ├─ [24953] 0xdAC17F958D2ee523a2206206994597C13D831ec7::approve(0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, 100000000000 [1e11])
+    │   │   ├─ emit Approval(owner: UsdtYieldVault: [0x5615dEB798BB3E4dFa0139dFa1b3D433Cc23b72f], spender: 0x87870Bca3F3fD6335C3F4ce8392D69350B4fA4E2, value: 100000000000 [1e11])
+    │   │   └─ ← [Stop]
+    │   └─ ← [Revert] EvmError: Revert
+    └─ ← [Revert] EvmError: Revert
+
+Backtrace:
+  at UsdtYieldVault.deposit
+  at VaultMainnetForkTest.test_DepositAgainstRealUsdt
+```
+
+Read the trace: `approve` itself returns `[Stop]` — it executed fine on-chain —
+and `deposit` reverts immediately after, on the decode. The existing
+`test_DepositSuppliesToAave` passes because `MockUSDT` is an OZ ERC20 whose
+`approve` returns a proper `bool`.
+
+### Fix
+
+Use `forceApprove` from the already-imported `SafeERC20`:
+
+```solidity
+usdt.forceApprove(address(aavePool), amount);
+```
+
+`forceApprove` handles both USDT quirks: the missing return value, and the
+`require` that blocks a non-zero→non-zero allowance change. Do not substitute a
+one-time infinite approval in the constructor — that also has to go through
+`forceApprove`, and it widens the blast radius if the pool address is ever
+wrong.
