+## F2 — Critical. The withdrawal fee is destroyed, not distributed
+
+### What is wrong
+
+`UsdtYieldVault.sol:110-117`:
+
+```solidity
+uint256 grossAssets = convertToAssets(shares);
+uint256 fee = (grossAssets * WITHDRAW_FEE_BPS) / BPS_DENOMINATOR;
+netAssets = grossAssets - fee;
+...
+totalAssetsStored -= grossAssets;      // full gross removed from accounting
+aavePool.withdraw(address(usdt), netAssets, msg.sender);   // only net removed from Aave
+```
+
+`totalAssetsStored` drops by `grossAssets`, but only `netAssets` actually leaves
+Aave. The `fee` stays in Aave as aUSDT that the vault's accounting no longer
+knows about.
+
+The NatSpec on line 15 and the README both state that withdrawal fees "stay
+supplied in Aave and accrue to the remaining shareholders". They do not. Because
+both `totalAssetsStored` and `totalShares` are reduced *proportionally*, the
+share price is exactly unchanged — remaining shareholders gain nothing. The fee
+accrues to no one. It is stranded, and there is no sweep, rescue, or
+`skim` function anywhere on the contract, so it is unrecoverable.
+
+At 30 bps this destroys 0.3% of every withdrawal, forever, and it compounds with
+every withdrawal the vault ever processes.
+
+Note that deposit fees *do* behave as documented (`totalAssetsStored += amount`
+on the full amount while shares are minted on the net) — which is likely why
+nobody noticed the withdrawal side does the opposite.
+
+### Evidence — the invariant search that found it
+
+`test/VaultInvariant.t.sol` states the property as an **equality**:
+`totalAssetsStored == aUSDT.balanceOf(vault)`. A one-sided `claims <= holdings`
+bound would have stayed green through this entire finding, because the vault
+ends up with a *surplus* it cannot see, not a shortfall.
+
+The fuzzer is pointed at `test/handlers/VaultHandler.sol`, which owns three
+funded and approved actors and bounds every input, so the sequences reach real
+vault states rather than bouncing off reverts. Revert rate is 0%.
+
